I’ve gone back through the sequence again, and I think the reconciliation view below gives a much better historical picture of what happened around the incident and should give you the link you were looking for between the monitoring, the incident being raised, and the data impact.

The source-side release was carried out after business hours on 30 July, during a lower-activity period. When the normal business day started on 31 July, the BICoE monitoring immediately highlighted that the expected MQS load had not landed correctly. That was the trigger for the investigation, and INC34965053 was raised once we confirmed the incoming MQS header format had changed and BICoE ingestion was no longer processing the messages in the expected structure.

At that point, the incoming traffic was diverted into a temporary/backup landing table so that we could continue retaining the incoming messages while the header issue was being addressed. Once the header handling was corrected, the retained data was then recovered and reprocessed through the normal flow.

The reason I’m using the reconciliation dashboard below is that the live monitoring dashboards are primarily designed for operational monitoring and retain only a relatively short history. The reconciliation dashboard is snapshot-based, so it captures the daily reconciliation position and keeps that history for a much longer period. That makes it much better for looking back at the original incident timeline.

You can see the difference starting around 30/31 July, which aligns with the release and the issue being identified on the morning of the 31st. The impact then continues through the recovery period. The much larger spike visible on 3 August was a separate volume event during the recovery period and should not be treated as the original trigger for the 31 July incident.

So the sequence the graph helps show is:

30 Jul after-hours release → 31 Jul monitoring identifies the load issue → INC34965053 raised → traffic diverted to backup landing while the header issue is fixed → recovery/reprocessing follows → separate larger volume spike on 3 Aug.

Hopefully this gives the Risk Control tester a clearer historical view of the incident and how the monitoring evidence ties back to the incident and business communication.
